claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:34:20 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
>> Research what other people are doing in this area.
>
> Each item is marked [F] for something I read in the source, or [I]
> for my inference.
>
> - [F] Packaged workflows are the norm now, and they ship as skills or
>   plugins rather than as CLAUDE.md text. Superpowers (Jesse Vincent)
>   is a plugin of skills chained as brainstorm, worktree, write plan,
>   execute plan, TDD, review [c]. GitHub's Spec Kit is spec-first as a
>   set of commands: constitution, specify, plan, tasks, implement [d].
>   Both are whole "build" implementations you could swap in or out.
>
> - [F] Plugins can be loaded for one session without installing them,
>   with `claude --plugin-dir <dir>`. A --plugin-dir copy overrides an
>   installed plugin with the same name, and enabledPlugins can turn one
>   off per scope [e].

Tell me more about plugins. Specifically, how can you implement plugins
in a monorepo? And how do you control what skills claude has available
to it?

I ask because I'm curious about different levels of formality. For
example, say I have two skills:

- build-by-commit
- build-by-spec

I could make those as claude skills. Now when I run it, I don't just
want to execute a specific one - I want to ensure claude doesn't know
about or have access to the other one. I basically just don't want it to
choose that one. In agent terms, on a given turn, build-by-commit is an
available tool whereas build-by-spec is not.

I can see how that would be pretty easy to do in a packaged tool. I
write the different skills, install them into place, and then have flags
on my tool that choose those specific plugin dirs.

Presumably you can do something similar when launching claude with
various flags. I guess I'm just unclear on how you can configure access
when tools are in the .claude/skills dir. Maybe in my case I don't
actually put it in .claude/skills and instead just put it under src and
launch claude with it?
